              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"2. Show that there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $(m, n)$ such that\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}\\n$$\\nis a positive integer.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"british_mo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Define a sequence of positive integers by\\n\\\\[\\na_0=1,\\\\qquad a_1=2,\\\\qquad a_{k+2}=4a_{k+1}-a_k-1\\\\quad(k\\\\ge0).\\n\\\\]\\nWe prove inductively that\\n\\\\[\\na_k^2+a_{k+1}^2+a_k+a_{k+1}=4a_ka_{k+1}. \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nFor \\\\(k=0\\\\), this is \\\\(1+4+1+2=8\\\\). Suppose (1) holds and put \\\\(b=a_{k+1}\\\\). As a quadratic in \\\\(x\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nx^2+(1-4b)x+b^2+b=0\\n\\\\]\\nhas the root \\\\(x=a_k\\\\). By Vieta's formula its other root is\\n\\\\[\\n4b-1-a_k=4a_{k+1}-a_k-1=a_{k+2}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the same equation holds for \\\\(x=a_{k+2}\\\\), and by symmetry\\n\\\\[\\na_{k+1}^2+a_{k+2}^2+a_{k+1}+a_{k+2}=4a_{k+1}a_{k+2}.\\n\\\\]\\nThis proves (1) for every \\\\(k\\\\).\\n\\nMoreover, the sequence is strictly increasing. Indeed, \\\\(a_1>a_0>0\\\\), and if \\\\(a_{k+1}>a_k>0\\\\), then, since the terms are integers, \\\\(a_k\\\\le a_{k+1}-1\\\\), so\\n\\\\[\\na_{k+2}-a_{k+1}=3a_{k+1}-a_k-1\\\\ge2a_{k+1}>0.\\n\\\\]\\nHence all \\\\(a_k\\\\) are positive and the ordered pairs \\\\((a_k,a_{k+1})\\\\) are distinct.\\n\\nFinally, dividing (1) by \\\\(a_ka_{k+1}>0\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{a_k+1}{a_{k+1}}+\\\\frac{a_{k+1}+1}{a_k}=4.\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers \\\\((m,n)\\\\) for which the given expression is the positive integer \\\\(4\\\\).\"}",
